tion/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "d7fbfa1c-696c-569c-8a32-b9f903615eb9" :question/math-academy-id "q-80696" :question/problem "Determine the vertical asymptotes of $f(x)=\\frac{4x}{x({x}^{2}+1)}.$\n\nSelect all that apply. If none apply, leave all boxes unchecked.\n\n- **I.** $x=-1$\n- **II.** $x=0$\n- **III.** $x=1$" :question/worked-solution "To find the vertical asymptotes of a rational function, we factor the numerator and denominator and cancel any common factors. Then, we set the denominator equal to zero and solve for $x.$\n\nFactoring the numerator and denominator and canceling any common factors, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x) & =\\frac{4x}{x({x}^{2}+1)} \\\\  & =\\frac{4\\cancel{x}}{\\cancel{x}({x}^{2}+1)} \\\\  & =\\frac{4}{{x}^{2}+1}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we set the denominator equal to zero:\n\n$$\n{x}^{2}+1=0\n$$\n\nThis equation does not have any real solutions. Therefore, $f(x)$ has no vertical asymptotes."} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186348439 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186172016 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "d3e3fdc8-67d0-5b46-9c6b-c1ec8d4ea24f" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying an Equation That Generates a Slope Field Using Asymptotes" :topic/_knowledge-points [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048885 :topic/id #uuid "c96ee93a-fd82-595c-824f-e11934066ffc" :topic/math-academy-id 3350 :topic/title "Analyzing Slope Fields for Nonautonomous Differential Equations"}]}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "12e92c50-de89-5487-b47a-949e97719584" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186469194} {:answer/id #uuid "091f0680-b029-5b93-8cf1-dd5427a12e16" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186469195}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "12e92c50-de89-5487-b47a-949e97719584" :answer