/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "50a7324b-9111-57a6-8ac7-64efd93f7ba4" :question/math-academy-id "q-80714" :question/problem "![](images/fd/fd7e9afcccbbb635d6bde139697aec0cbaa4bad486ea8b098e96f74bfbefebc9.png)\n\nWhich of the following equations could have generated the slope field shown above?\n\n- ${y}^{′}=\\frac{1}{y}$\n- ${y}^{′}=\\frac{1}{x}$\n- ${y}^{′}=\\frac{2}{y-1}$\n\nSelect all that apply." :question/worked-solution "![](images/28/28c4751694caf50f08f73d7ccf3525877e6679901d49854951810f550a65ede2.png)\n\n \n\nNotice the following properties of the given slope field:\n\n- First, the slope lines on the same horizontal line are all the same. This means that the slope does not vary with $x.$ In other words, ${y}^{′}$ is a function of $y$ only.\n\n- Second, the slopes become vertical as $y\\to 1.$\n\nWith that in mind, let's consider each equation.\n\n- Equation I does *not* give the required behavior. The right-hand side of ${y}^{′}=\\frac{1}{y}$ is a function of $y$ only, but\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=\\frac{1}{y}\\to 1\\,\\text{as}\\,y\\to 1.\n$$\n\n- Equation II does *not* give the required behavior. The right-hand side of ${y}^{′}=\\frac{1}{x}$ is a function of $x$ only.\n\n- Equation III gives the required behavior. The right-hand side of ${y}^{′}=\\frac{2}{y-1}$ is a function of $y$ only, and \n\n$$\n|{y}^{′}|=|\\frac{2}{y-1}|\\to \\infty \\,\\text{as}\\,y\\to 1.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"III only.\""} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186348444 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124450 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "0b15f59e-ef74-5fa2-9af9-448fdcbbbd3d" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Possible Pairs of First-Order Partial Derivatives" :topic/_knowledge-points [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049696 :topic/id #uuid "0bfad6b0-12ee-58d9-8324-1f301647b82f" :topic/math-academy-id 1957 :topic/title "Equality of Mixed Partial Derivatives"}]}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "fcaa57c3-7261-59eb-a60b-ad52b99906e3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186469200} {:answer/id #uuid "83d68cbc-4921-5172-a9ca-3187aa298c05" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186469201}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "fcaa57c3-7261-59eb-a60b-ad52b99906e3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186469200} :answer-field/id #uuid "f2f899af-df2d-5c87-a6bd-3231948aebed" :answer-field/key "I" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186470212} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0bcb56e4-bbc1-5fb2-85ee-b31815b63b3c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186469202} {:answer/id #uuid "61ab9279-cb43-56c4-8210-357418c82d9f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186469203}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "61ab9279-cb43-56c4-8210-357418c82d9f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186469203} :answer-field/id #uuid "dc9282ed-cbd0-5b59-9c94-2cfe922f8c6e" :answer-field/key "II" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186470213} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "6aa29ade-14c2-5462-a161-80eb15611ae7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186469204} {:answer/id #uuid "642b209a-f515-5805-a84c-4448a5ebec64" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186469205}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "642b209a-f515-5805-a84c-4448a5ebec64" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186469205} :answer-field/id #uuid "0aa84786-f368-590a-a9c2-7799230ff6e0" :answer-field/key "III" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186470214}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "22141837-7250-500e-8f49-e3a949023800" :question/math-academy-id "q-80753" :question/problem "Suppose that the function $f(x,y)$ and all of its first and second-order partial derivatives are continuous on an open set $U.$ Which of the following pairs could represent the first-order partial derivatives of $f(x,y)?$\n- **I.** ${f}_{x}(x,y)=2x{y}^{2}-y$ and ${f}_{y}(x,y)=2{x}^{2}y-2x$\n- **II.** ${f}_{x}(x,y)=yx{e}^{x}+{y}^{2}$ and ${f}_{y}(x,y)=(x-1){e}^{x}+2xy$\n- **III.** ${f}_{x}(x,y)=x-y$ and ${f}_{y}(x,y)=\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡y}-x$\n\nSelect all that apply." :question/worked-solution "Since the function and its first and second-order partial derivatives are continuous on an open set $U,$ the mixed partial derivatives must be equal, that is, ${f}_{xy}(x,y)={f}_{yx}(x,y).$ Let's check if this condition is true for each of our pairs of first-order partial derivatives.\n\n- For the first pair, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{xy}(x,y) & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}(2x{y}^{2}-y)=4xy-1 \\\\ {f}_{yx}(x,y) & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(2{x}^{2}y-2x)=4xy-2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAs ${f}_{xy}(x,y)\\ne {f}_{yx}(x,y)$, the first pair cannot represent the first-order partial derivatives of $f(x,y).$\n\n- For the second pair, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{xy}(x,y) & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}(yx{e}^{x}+{y}^{2})=x{e}^{x}+2y \\\\ {f}_{yx}(x,y) & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}((x-1){e}^{x}+2xy)=x{e}^{x}+2y.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAs ${f}_{xy}(x,y)={f}_{yx}(x,y)$, the second pair could represent the first-order partial derivatives of $f(x,y).$\n\n- For the third pair, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{xy}(x,y) & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}(x-y)=-1 \\\\ {f}_{yx}(x,y) & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡y}-x)=-1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAs ${f}_{xy}(x,y)={f}_{yx}(x,y)$,  the third pair could represent the first-order partial derivatives of $f(x,y).$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is 'II and III only'."} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186348445 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124450 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "0b15f59e-ef74-5fa2-9af9-448fdcbbbd3d" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Possible Pairs of First-Order Partial Derivatives" :topic/_knowledge-points [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049696 :topic/id #uuid "0bfad6b0-12ee-58d9-8324-1f301647b82f" :topic/math-academy-id 1957 :topic/title "Equality of Mixed Partial Derivatives"}]}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "ad486eb1-3af5-599d-a9fa-a159211f22e0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186469206} {:answer/id #uuid "34be447a-67c4-5a73-8480-4c9e222a6cf7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186469207}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "ad486eb1-3af5-599d-a9fa-a159211f22e0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186469206} :answer-field/id #uuid "25cbfe12-d294-5245-8577-5393288ad9f4" :answer-field/key "I" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186470215} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "b396de7c-837a-5a29-89f1-49cbd1530aca" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186469208} {:answer/id #uuid "8cec73c4-a0c4-5d0f-b9fe-a203948ac871" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186469209}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "8cec73c4-a0c4-5d0f-b9fe-a203948ac871" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186469209} :answer-field/id #uuid "a8af40fc-fffa-522e-a59f-f12c36507ded" :answer-field/key "II" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186470216} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "dcdf7232-c17f-5a4f-aed3-4cd3d5ebaa84" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186469210} {:answer/id #uuid "45e16277-28a2-510b-88f0-c7b6217947e0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186469211}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "45e16277-28a2-510b-88f0-c7b6217947e0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186469211} :answer-field/id #uuid "f6521383-885b-5c99-af14-d69ae9a433ee" :answer-field/key "III" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186470217}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "0fedf8ed-0c48-5105-b489-f0efda76d273" :question/math-academy-id "q-80759" :question/problem "Suppose that the function $f(x,y)$ and all of its first and second-order partial derivatives are continuous on an open set $U.$ Which of the following pairs could represent the first-order partial derivatives of $f(x,y)?$\n- **I.** ${f}_{x}(x,y)={e}^{-2x}-y$ and ${f}_{y}(x,y)={e}^{-y}-2x$\n- **II.** ${f}_{x}(x,y)={e}^{x}\\operatorname{cos}⁡x$ and ${f}_{y}(x,y)=5$\n- **III.** ${f}_{x}(x,y)=2{y}^{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡xy+{y}^{2}$ and ${f}_{y}(x,y)=2\\operatorname{sin}⁡xy+2xy\\operatorname{cos}⁡xy+2xy$\n\nSelect all that apply." :question/worked-solution "Since the function and its first and second-order partial derivatives are continuous on an open set $U,$ the mixed partial derivatives must be equal, that is, ${f}_{xy}(x,y)={f}_{yx}(x,y).$ Let's check if this condition is true for each of our pairs of first-order partial derivatives.\n\n- For the first pair, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{xy}(x,y) & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}({e}^{-2x}-y)=-1 \\\\ {f}_{yx}(x,y) & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}({e}^{-y}-2x)=-2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAs ${f}_{xy}(x,y)\\ne {f}_{yx}(x,y)$, the first pair cannot represent the first-order partial derivatives of $f(x,y).$\n\n- For the second pair, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{xy}(x,y) & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}({e}^{x}\\operatorname{cos}⁡x)=0 \\\\ {f}_{yx}(x,y) & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(5)=0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAs ${f}_{xy}(x,y)={f}_{yx}(x,y)$, the second pair could represent the first-order partial derivatives of $f(x,y).$\n\n- For the third pair, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{xy}(x,y) & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}(2{y}^{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡xy+{y}^{2})=4y\\operatorname{cos}⁡xy-2x{y}^{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡xy+2y \\\\ {f}_{yx}(x,y) & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(2\\operatorname{sin}⁡xy+2xy\\operatorname{cos}⁡xy+2xy) \\\\  & =2y\\operatorname{cos}⁡xy+2y\\operatorname{cos}⁡xy-2x{y}^{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡xy+2y \\\\  & =4y\\operatorname{cos}⁡xy-2x{y}^{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡xy+2y.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAs ${f}_{xy}(x,y)={f}_{yx}(x,y)$,  the third pair could represent the first-order partial derivatives of $f(x,y).$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is 'II and III only'." :step/_content [{:activity/_steps {:activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049696 :topic/id #uuid "0bfad6b0-12ee-58d9-8324-1f301647b82f" :topic/math-academy-id 1957 :topic/title "Equality of Mixed Partial Derivatives"} :activity/title "Equality of Mixed Partial Derivatives" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :activity.type/review} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186409637} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186409653} {:activity/_steps {:activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049696 :topic/id #uuid "0bfad6b0-12ee-58d9-8324-1f301647b82f" :topic/math-academy-id 1957 :topic/title "Equality of Mixed Partial Derivatives"} :activity/title "Equality of Mixed Partial Derivatives" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :activity.type/review} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186481568} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186481744}]} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186351159 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186054631 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "9b7f1d12-3335-5dd7-bf36-e07077d0c45d" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Subsets of Special Sets" :topic/_knowledge-points [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050923 :topic/id #uuid "723d1f9c-b696-511c-b5e5-88c045fe7609" :topic/math-academy-id 50 :topic/title "Subsets"}]}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "a7db507f-6831-59b7-b41e-05d9eef5583f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186469212} {:answer/id #uuid "251af1ac-478a-5604-94e1-b76f0d26a56a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186469213}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "a7db507f-6831-59b7-b41e-05d9eef5583f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186469212} :answer-field/id #uuid "59fe37a6-0f54-5ba5-9c56-30ba40994227" :answer-field/key "I" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186470218} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "793eed01-8073-5e64-b90a-7a2540b61586" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186469214} {:answer/id #uuid "7edd5234-d257-5ce3-88a2-54281ac4f491" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186469215}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "7edd5234-d257-5ce3-88a2-54281ac4f491" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186469215} :answer-field/id #uuid "24f9ff0d-a4aa-5bb9-aed2-f1c0bdc39dc5" :answer-field/key "II" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186470219} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "e904b9f8-2f23-5838-99f4-e2851e235bd1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186469216} {:answer/id #uuid "4f9738a2-916a-5ae9-ae22-3b7231ed04f6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186469217}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "e904b9f8-2f23-5838-99f4-e2851e235bd1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186469216} :answer-field/id #uuid "a2f6331b-9d7b-5794-938e-f94bc7411649" :answer-field/key "III" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186470220}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "94ae6b8c-148f-5682-9bab-a030784afd5c" :question/math-academy-id "q-80812" :question/problem "Which of the following are subsets of ${Q}^{+}$  (the set of positive rational numbers)?\n- **I.** ${Q}_{0}^{+}$ (the set of non-negative rational numbers)\n- **II.** $N$\n- **III.** $Z$\n\nSelect all that apply." :question/worked-solution "$A$ is a subset of $B$ if every element of $A$ is an element of $B.$\n\nWith that in mind, let's inspect the given choices.\n\n- ${Q}_{0}^{+}$ is not a subset of ${Q}^{+}.$ For example, $0\\in {Q}_{0}^{+}$ but $0\\notin {Q}^{+}.$\n\n- $N$ is a subset of ${Q}^{+}.$ Indeed, every natural number is also a positive rational number.\n\n- $Z$ is not a subset of ${Q}^{+}.$ For example, $-1\\in Z$ but $-1\\notin {Q}^{+}.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II only\"."} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186351166 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186054631 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "9b7f1d12-3335-5dd7-bf36-e07077d0c45d" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Subsets of Special Sets" :topic/_knowledge-points [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050923 :topic/id #uuid "723d1f9c-b696-511c-b5e5-88c045fe7609" :topic/math-academy-id 50 :topic/title "Subsets"}]}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "a0cf0642-f1bf-5c08-bf97-a1c252e3244d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186469218} {:answer/id #uuid "e3c2bd5d-9217-50c8-903f-8f1030630836" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186469219}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "a0cf0642-f1bf-5c08-bf97-a1c252e3244d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186469218} :answer-field/id #uuid "645da03c-b9f6-542c-a343-1ec6790540e2" :answer-field/key "I" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186470221} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "4b0e65fb-08ed-5e06-a807-8e5e643d1b40" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186469220} {:answer/id #uuid "82da2f9d-2f7c-5247-b22b-788b4214e6c3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186469221}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "4b0e65fb-08ed-5e06-a807-8e5e643d1b40" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186469220} :answer-field/id #uuid "1de1b366-1262-54b5-892c-e550f32f4903" :answer-field/key "II" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186470222} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "799dc097-9cde-5b38-bb7b-56ed200dd1e1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186469222} {:answer/id #uuid "5999b963-e764-5af5-8933-6a0bbec9d0ee" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186469223}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "5999b963-e764-5af5-8933-6a0bbec9d0ee" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186469223} :answer-field/id #uuid "0dad78fb-1306-528c-a5a8-29056d115f6c" :answer-field/key "III" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186470223}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "f854e867-6b9f-52a2-9a74-9314a61b1db2" :question/math-academy-id "q-80829" :question/problem "Which of the following are subsets of ${R}^{-}$ (the set of negative real numbers)?\n- **I.** ${Z}_{0}^{-}$ (the set of non-positive integers)\n- **II.** $N$\n- **III.** ${Q}^{-}$  (the set of negative rational numbers)\n\nSelect all that apply." :question/worked-solution "$A$ is a subset of $B$ if every element of $A$ is an element of $B.$\n\nWith that in mind, let's inspect the given choices.\n\n- ${Z}_{0}^{-}$ is *not* a subset of ${R}^{-}.$ For example, $0\\in {Z}_{0}^{-}$ but $0\\notin {R}^{-}.$\n\n- $N$ is not  a subset of ${R}^{-}.$ For example, $1\\in N$ but $1\\notin {R}^{-}.$\n\n- ${Q}^{-}$ is a subset of ${R}^{-}.$ Indeed, every negative rational number is also a negative real number.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"III only\"."} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186351168 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186054632 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "495f3da0-849a-589d-ac88-26d6825445e4" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying True Statements About Subsets" :topic/_knowledge-points [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050923 :topic/id #uuid "723d1f9c-b696-511c-b5e5-88c045fe7609" :topic/math-academy-id 50 :topic/title "Subsets"}]}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "dc8ced0a-ba6f-5ad7-88c0-9ebf46c62208" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186451795} {:answer/id #uuid "342e13e3-9800-53cf-9366-d3c87a489b4e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186451796}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "dc8ced0a-ba6f-5ad7-88c0-9ebf46c62208" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186451795} :answer-field/id #uuid "571944e4-b535-51ab-92d3-6cdb09493a7a" :answer-field/key "I" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186452745} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1126e9e0-8232-5d35-8038-8bf328ded170" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186451797} {:answer/id #uuid "615a2768-0749-5412-a7db-f09acc474a5b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186451798}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "615a2768-0749-5412-a7db-f09acc474a5b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186451798} :answer-field/id #uuid "abf90b77-7bc6-5618-b11c-374e8a2add6f" :answer-field/key "II" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186452746} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "b212baa9-6aa5-509c-9b0f-258b4ef85f55" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186451799} {:answer/id #uuid "73feb374-98b9-585c-8aba-c90f06e91ed8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186451800}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "73feb374-98b9-585c-8aba-c90f06e91ed8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186451800} :answer-field/id #uuid "38c2d820-d8fc-578d-a1ab-2c25f2897ba2" :answer-field/key "III" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186452747}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "1f0cc03e-26f5-51bc-b8ad-bdb1b8cf7d61" :question/math-academy-id "q-80832" :question/problem "Which of the following statements are true for the sets $A={x\\in N:1\\le x<6}$ and $B={x\\in N:3x<9}?$\n\nSelect all that apply.\n\n- **I.** $A⊆B$\n- **II.** $B⊆A$\n- **III.** $A⊈B$" :question/worked-solution "First, we must find the elements of the sets $A$ and $B.$\n\n- The elements of the set $A$ are all natural numbers greater than or equal to $1$ but less than $6.$ So,\n\n$$\nA={1,2,3,4,5}.\n$$\n\n- On the other hand, the elements of the set $B$ are natural numbers which satisfy the inequality $3x<9.$ So,\n\n$$\nB={1,2}.\n$$\n\nNow, let's inspect the given statements.\n\n- $A⊆B$ is false, while $A⊈B$ is true. For example, $5\\in A$ but $5\\notin B.$\n\n- $B⊆A$ is true. Every element in the set $B$ is an element of the set $A.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II and III only\"."} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186351170 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186054632 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "495f3da0-849a-589d-ac88-26d6825445e4" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying True Statements About Subsets" :topic/_knowledge-points [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050923 :topic/id #uuid "723d1f9c-b696-511c-b5e5-88c045fe7609" :topic/math-academy-id 50 :topic/title "Subsets"}]}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "c94151ca-d0d0-5c0a-9142-2d9e3bbdf24c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186451801} {:answer/id #uuid "b63ee5cf-27b9-5337-82e1-5f492db4a72e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186451802}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "c94151ca-d0d0-5c0a-9142-2d9e3bbdf24c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186451801} :answer-field/id #uuid "64224f0b-584b-59b9-9aa9-96918c898c62" :answer-field/key "I" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186452748} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "ec08aaf9-9d96-5ea6-b0b4-c472fe416aa0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186451803} {:answer/id #uuid "a321fa62-5e61-53ab-94ec-ba3ba9892960" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186451804}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "a321fa62-5e61-53ab-94ec-ba3ba9892960" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186451804} :answer-field/id #uuid "a51f3693-e502-5224-bb23-05134e129548" :answer-field/key "II" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186452749} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "09c3a9bc-5d80-5f5f-b63c-998493639335" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186451805} {:answer/id #uuid "67243169-62b8-5e91-b25a-6a53cecb1b2a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186451806}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "09c3a9bc-5d80-5f5f-b63c-998493639335" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186451805} :answer-field/id #uuid "3d09f76c-3a00-52e9-82e2-d4ddd8f9c8a5" :answer-field/key "III" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186452750}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "c643dbca-796e-5e8a-ae4e-2697e41e25d4" :question/math-academy-id "q-80837" :question/problem "Which of the following statements are true for the sets $A={x\\in {Z}^{+}∣x\\le 3}$ and $B={x\\in Z∣0\\le x<3}?$\n\nSelect all that apply.\n\n- **I.** $A⊆B$\n- **II.** $B⊈A$\n- **III.** $∅⊈B$" :question/worked-solution "First, we must find the elements of the sets $A$ and $B.$\n\n- The elements of the set $A$ are all positive integers that satisfy the inequality $x\\le 3.$ So, we have \n\n$$\nA={1,2,3}.\n$$\n\n- On the other hand, the elements of the set $B$ are all integers that satisfy the inequality $0\\le x<3.$ So, we have\n\n$$\nB={0,1,2}.\n$$\n\nNow, let's inspect the given statements.\n\n- $A⊆B$ is false. For example, $3\\in A$ but $3\\notin B.$\n\n- $B⊈A$ is true. For example, $0\\in B$ but $0\\notin A.$\n\n- $∅⊈B$ is false. The null set $∅={}$ is a subset of *every* set.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II only.\""} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186351173 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186054632 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "495f3da0-849a-589d-ac88-26d6825445e4" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying True Statements About Subsets" :topic/_knowledge-points [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050923 :topic/id #uuid "723d1f9c-b696-511c-b5e5-88c045fe7609" :topic/math-academy-id 50 :topic/title "Subsets"}]}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "dd183097-a404-5feb-952a-2387b100d23e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186451807} {:answer/id #uuid "94030ec2-e77c-5e81-83f3-5026503525a4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186451808}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "94030ec2-e77c-5e81-83f3-5026503525a4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186451808} :answer-field/id #uuid "16a854c1-f276-5aa3-841d-8cfe4d3ed38b" :answer-field/key "I" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186452751} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "a18f6937-d520-5613-9309-e9c169d33c50" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186451809} {:answer/id #uuid "46aa31ca-9b95-5fbc-b4ec-a1024e21fa1e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186451810}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "46aa31ca-9b95-5fbc-b4ec-a1024e21fa1e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186451810} :answer-field/id #uuid "dfc2a7c7-14a4-5bf0-87d3-bd29de089111" :answer-field/key "II" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186452752} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "4170e1ed-77e4-53c4-92ee-6bff83a161af" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186451811} {:answer/id #uuid "27506552-dcc7-5346-a765-924cf632d717" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186451812}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "4170e1ed-77e4-53c4-92ee-6bff83a161af" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186451811} :answer-field/id #uuid "11e5ec35-2b0c-515a-9a19-0eb9259b534c" :answer-field/key "III" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186452753} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "d211362a-74a6-5813-bdcd-dcd0ca5d5678" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186451813} {:answer/id #uuid "4b7675a8-0e49-5dd3-83c4-190e40851363" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186451814}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "4b7675a8-0e49-5dd3-83c4-190e40851363" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186451814} :answer-field/id #uuid "01e8cc2e-5ff4-54ad-9049-8173ff928c64" :answer-field/key "IV" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186452754}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "13666e1e-c022-5b9a-b071-b81546a1b503" :question/math-academy-id "q-80845" :question/problem "Which of the following statements are true for the sets $A={n\\in Z∣n+1\\ge -1}$ and $B={n\\in Z∣n-2\\ge -4}?$\n\nSelect all that apply.\n\n- **I.** $A⊆B$\n- **II.** $B⊆A$\n- **III.** $B⊈A$\n- **IV.** $B⊇A$" :question/worked-solution "First, we must find the elements of the sets $A$ and $B.$\n\n- The elements of the set $A$ are all the integer numbers that satisfy the inequality $n+1\\ge -1,$ that is to say, $n\\ge -2.$ So, we have \n\n$$\nA={-2,-1,0,1,2,3,4,5,6,…}.\n$$\n\n- On the other hand, the elements of the set $B$ are all integers that satisfy the inequality $n-2\\ge -4,$ That is to say, $n\\ge -2.$ So, we have \n\n$$\nB={-2,-1,0,1,2,3,4,5,6,…}.\n$$\n\nNow, let's inspect the given statements.\n\n- $A⊆B$ is true. Every element in the set $A$ is an element of the set $B.$\n\n- $B⊆A$ is true, while $B⊈A$ is false. Every element in the set $B$ is an element of the set $A.$\n\n- $B⊇A$ is equivalent to $A⊆B,$ which is true.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I, II, and IV only\"."} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186351223 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186133781 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "ed5c8af7-e617-5e8c-be01-45a26eb17d4d" :knowledge-point/title "Determining Whether Sets are Orthonormal" :topic/_knowledge-points [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049811 :topic/id #uuid "157ebcd0-6389-529a-a695-53136d4482c5" :topic/math-academy-id 2103 :topic/title "Orthogonal Sets in Euclidean Spaces"}]}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "4f9ec959-6c9a-57e1-ae0a-6a076a7d5def" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186469224} {:answer/id #uuid "2d4ab090-ec29-550d-ba58-e9d28247fdd2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186469225}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "4f9ec959-6c9a-57e1-ae0a-6a076a7d5def" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186469224} :answer-field/id #uuid "5af42925-d1d8-558c-98a2-b6afb6006d99" :answer-field/key "I" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186470224} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "f85509fa-72b0-5356-9281-0306a2883679" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186469226} {:answer/id #uuid "8d077489-acb7-511c-ba14-a31d55734b32" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186469227}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "8d077489-acb7-511c-ba14-a31d55734b32" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186469227} :answer-field/id #uuid "6cf7eb21-0d7a-553e-8a84-78201bbb4178" :answer-field/key "II" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186470225} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "bd229fc2-3cb9-5263-8bc2-1ad4e67230df" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186469228} {:answer/id #uuid "d68af363-ffa3-5154-bebb-0a3e9fd71962" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186469229}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "d68af363-ffa3-5154-bebb-0a3e9fd71962" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186469229} :answer-field/id #uuid "d5130a91-d1f8-5b78-832a-6465f79297b4" :answer-field/key "III" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186470226}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "25a9c5a2-c2b4-580a-a454-817fa98a8c5d" :question/math-academy-id "q-81109" :question/problem "Which of the following sets are orthonormal?\n\nSelect all that apply.\n\n- **I.** $U=\\{\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ -5 \\\\ -2\\end{bmatrix},\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix},\\begin{bmatrix}-2 \\\\ -2 \\\\ 4\\end{bmatrix}\\}$\n\n- **II.** $V=\\{\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{13}} \\\\ \\frac{3}{\\sqrt{13}}\\end{bmatrix},\\begin{bmatrix}-\\frac{3}{\\sqrt{13}} \\\\ \\frac{2}{\\sqrt{13}}\\end{bmatrix}\\}$\n\n- **III.** $W=\\{\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{6}} \\\\ \\frac{2}{\\sqrt{6}} \\\\ \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{6}}\\end{bmatrix},\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3}} \\\\ -\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3}} \\\\ \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3}}\\end{bmatrix}\\}$" :question/worked-solution "Let's denote the vectors of the given sets as \n$\\{{u}_{1},{u}_{2},{u}_{3}\\},$ \n$\\{{v}_{1},{v}_{2}\\},$ and \n$\\{{w}_{1},{w}_{2}\\},$ respectively. \n\nThe sets $U,V,$ and $W$ are orthonormal if the following conditions on each set hold:\n\n- $\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}{u}_{1}\\cdot {u}_{2} &=0 \\\\ {u}_{1}\\cdot {u}_{3} &=0 \\\\ {u}_{2}\\cdot {u}_{3} &=0\\end{aligned}\\right.\\,\\text{and}\\,\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}{u}_{1}\\cdot {u}_{1} &=1 \\\\ {u}_{2}\\cdot {u}_{2} &=1 \\\\ {u}_{3}\\cdot {u}_{3} &=1.\\end{aligned}\\right.$\n\n- ${v}_{1}\\cdot {v}_{2}=0\\,\\text{and}\\,\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}{v}_{1}\\cdot {v}_{1} &=1 \\\\ {v}_{2}\\cdot {v}_{2} &=1.\\end{aligned}\\right.$\n\n- $\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}{w}_{1}\\cdot {w}_{2} &=0 \\\\ {w}_{1}\\cdot {w}_{3} &=0 \\\\ {w}_{2}\\cdot {w}_{3} &=0\\end{aligned}\\right.\\,\\text{and}\\,\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}{w}_{1}\\cdot {w}_{1} &=1 \\\\ {w}_{2}\\cdot {w}_{2} &=1 \\\\ {w}_{3}\\cdot {w}_{3} &=1.\\end{aligned}\\right.$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our sets in turn.\n\n- For the set $U,$ notice that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{u}_{1}\\cdot {u}_{1} & =\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ -5 \\\\ -2\\end{bmatrix}\\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ -5 \\\\ -2\\end{bmatrix}=1+25+4=30\\ne 1 & \\times \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, $U$ is not an orthonormal set.\n\n- For the set $V,$ we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{v}_{1}\\cdot {v}_{2} & =\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{13}} \\\\ \\frac{3}{\\sqrt{13}}\\end{bmatrix}\\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}-\\frac{3}{\\sqrt{13}} \\\\ \\frac{2}{\\sqrt{13}}\\end{bmatrix}=-\\frac{6}{13}+\\frac{6}{13}=0 & ✓ \\\\ {v}_{1}\\cdot {v}_{1} & =\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{13}} \\\\ \\frac{3}{\\sqrt{13}}\\end{bmatrix}\\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{13}} \\\\ \\frac{3}{\\sqrt{13}}\\end{bmatrix}=\\frac{4}{13}+\\frac{9}{13}=1 & ✓ \\\\ {v}_{2}\\cdot {v}_{2} & =\\begin{bmatrix}-\\frac{3}{\\sqrt{13}} \\\\ \\frac{2}{\\sqrt{13}}\\end{bmatrix}\\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}-\\frac{3}{\\sqrt{13}} \\\\ \\frac{2}{\\sqrt{13}}\\end{bmatrix}=\\frac{9}{13}+\\frac{4}{13}=1 & ✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the set $V$ is orthonormal.\n\n- For the set $W,$ we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{w}_{1}\\cdot {w}_{2} & =\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{6}} \\\\ \\frac{2}{\\sqrt{6}} \\\\ \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{6}}\\end{bmatrix}\\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3}} \\\\ -\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3}} \\\\ \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3}}\\end{bmatrix}=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{18}}-\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{18}}+\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{18}}=0 & ✓ \\\\ {w}_{1}\\cdot {w}_{1} & =\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{6}} \\\\ \\frac{2}{\\sqrt{6}} \\\\ \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{6}}\\end{bmatrix}\\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{6}} \\\\ \\frac{2}{\\sqrt{6}} \\\\ \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{6}}\\end{bmatrix}=\\frac{1}{6}+\\frac{4}{6}+\\frac{1}{6}=1 & ✓ \\\\ {w}_{2}\\cdot {w}_{2} & =\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3}} \\\\ -\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3}} \\\\ \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3}}\\end{bmatrix}\\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3}} \\\\ -\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3}} \\\\ \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3}}\\end{bmatrix}=\\frac{1}{3}+\\frac{1}{3}+\\frac{1}{3}=1 & ✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the set $W$ is orthonormal.\n\nTherefore, we conclude that the correct answer is \"$V$ and $W$ only\"."} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186351244 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124654 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "4ca319c5-9f62-51e3-b670-611fc8a8daaa" :knowledge-point/title "Classifying a Critical Point Using the Second Partial Derivatives Test" :topic/_knowledge-points [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186051048 :topic/id #uuid "7d157e41-c50b-570b-8b5b-b8a5a0d03163" :topic/math-academy-id 1944 :topic/title "The Second Partial Derivatives Test"}]}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "388c46a0-2156-5b94-b255-eb7d8c824a71" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 