 0 \\\\ 0 & -1\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nTo find the image of $S$ under the action of ${R}_{\\pi },$ we first create a matrix $X$ containing all of the vertices of $S:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}X & =\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 3 & 4 \\\\ 1 & 3 & 4\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we compute the image of $X$ under ${R}_{\\pi }$ by calculating the matrix product $RX:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}RX & =\\begin{bmatrix}-1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -1\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 3 & 4 \\\\ 1 & 3 & 4\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}-2 & -3 & -4 \\\\ -1 & -3 & -4\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the coordinates of the respective vertices of ${S}^{′}$ are $(-2,-1),(-3,-3),(-4,-4).$\n\nSo, the correct answer is \"I and III only.\""} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186354291 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186092223 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "1b86558a-4c99-5ccb-a100-719692a056f4" :knowledge-point/title "Using a Matrix That Represents a Rotation of 90 Degrees" :topic/_knowledge-points [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048712 :topic/id #uuid "b784cbd9-6f0d-5689-9a0e-6e19dfca34c7" :topic/math-academy-id 931 :topic/title "Right-Angle Rotations as Linear Transformations"}]}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "6447923e-3191-5c08-b560-016a9cca81ca" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186469362} {:answer/id #uuid "0cc433cf-3e13-565e-b11d-47ebc6009d0b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186469363}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "0cc433cf-3e13-565e-b11d-47ebc6009d0b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186469363} :answer-field/id #uuid "0cdb2ef9-a7f4-5197-a4be-9be3babb8318" :answer-field/key "I" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592