answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186469475} :answer-field/id #uuid "4d6e2154-d58b-586e-a921-63d267d916c2" :answer-field/key "III" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186470349}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "34701839-8ff2-5e14-88d5-939b8e4e71de" :question/math-academy-id "q-87160" :question/problem "Which of the following are continuous random variables?\n- **I.** The volume of milk produced by a particular cow on a randomly selected day, measured in liters\n- **II.** The outcome of rolling a $6$-sided die\n- **III.** The distance that a randomly selected runner can run in $30$ minutes\n\nSelect all that apply." :question/worked-solution "A random variable is a variable whose value is determined by the outcome of a trial or experiment. A *continuous* random variable is a random variable whose possible values are represented by an interval of real numbers.\n\nCommon examples of continuous random variables are shown in the diagram below.\n\n![](images/05/05e3d893f6c069dce5b51dc7d9fa5830b44bca58fa071009548fde20ebe92c19.png)\n\nWith that in mind, let's consider each of the given statements.\n\n- Statement I describes a continuous random variable. Its value is determined by the result of an experiment, and its possible values correspond to the real numbers in the interval $(0,\\infty ).$\n\n- Statement II does *not* describe a continuous random variable. Its value is determined by the result of an experiment, but its possible values are the integers $1,2,3,…,6,$ which do not correspond to an interval of real numbers.\n\n- Statement III describes a continuous random variable. Its value is determined by the result of an experiment, and its possible values correspond to the real numbers in the interval $(0,\\infty ).$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and III only.\""} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186354632 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186157397 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "3a247336-9bc8-5799-9956-e3de188ce2d2" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Integrals That Evaluate to Zero" :topic/_knowledge-points [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048495 :topic/id #uuid "a4841cae-d95f-5e07-960c-2030a42a2568" :topic/math-academy-id 2975 :topic/title "Evaluating Definite Integrals Using Symmetry"}]}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "c4290d91-204d-56a0-9987-3c5e660b7bcc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186469476} {:answer/id #uuid "7edb0a43-f3f2-57e9-aabb-7339e723844c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186469477}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "7edb0a43-f3f2-57e9-aabb-7339e723844c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186469477} :answer-field/id #uuid "5f4405f6-a235-58a4-9c4d-f1c15efcb26c" :answer-field/key "I" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186470350} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "942baa87-51cf-5c27-bbe2-b4d72e9a1c36" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186469478} {:answer/id #uuid "6ca043e7-9abf-5a74-ad98-784c787973ea" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186469479}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "942baa87-51cf-5c27-bbe2-b4d72e9a1c36" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186469478} :answer-field/id #uuid "1a0c8103-5b3e-5f3b-98cc-6c07404b52bb" :answer-field/key "II" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186470351} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "f5228a88-de94-53c9-8036-7395a8d03a7c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186469480} {:answer/id #uuid "dd9cb4a1-c8af-5b14-8e4c-5217af5953bf" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186469481}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "dd9cb4a1-c8af-5b14-8e4c-5217af5953bf" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186469481} :answer-field/id #uuid "d7bc4848-1177-5718-82e6-6482054cb9b1" :answer-field/key "III" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186470352}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "02a56550-64c7-56dd-a9e8-7fca3f195d30" :question/math-academy-id "q-88208" :question/problem "Which of the following integrals evaluates to zero due to symmetry?\n- **I.** ${\\int }_{-5}^{5}x\\operatorname{cos}⁡({x}^{2})\\,\\text{d}x$\n- **II.** ${\\int }_{-2}^{2}x\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x+1)\\,\\text{d}x$\n- **III.** ${\\int }_{-\\pi }^{\\pi }({x}^{3}-2x)\\,\\text{d}x$\n\nSelect all that apply." :question/worked-solution "Recall the following:\n\n- a function $f(x)$ is *even* if $f(-x)=f(x)$\n\n- a function $f(x)$ is *odd* if $f(-x)=-f(x)$\n\nMoreover, for any odd function $f(x),$ the graph of $y=f(x)$ has rotational symmetry of order $2$ about the origin, so we have\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{-a}^{a}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x=0.\n$$\n\nSo, to determine which integrals evaluate to zero due to symmetry, we have to determine which integrands are odd functions.\n\n- The function $f(x)=x\\operatorname{cos}⁡({x}^{2})$ is an odd function:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(-x) & =(-x)\\operatorname{cos}⁡((-x)^{2}) \\\\  & =-x\\operatorname{cos}⁡({x}^{2}) \\\\  & =-f(x)\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, by symmetry, we can conclude that\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{-5}^{5}x\\operatorname{cos}⁡({x}^{2})\\,\\text{d}x=0.\n$$\n\n- The function $g(x)=x\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x+1)$ is *not* an odd function:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}g(-x) & =(-x)\\operatorname{sin}⁡(-x+1) \\\\  & =-x\\operatorname{sin}⁡(1-x) \\\\  & \\ne -g(x)\\,\\times \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- The function $h(x)={x}^{3}-2x$ is an odd function:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}h(-x) & =(-x)^{3}-2(-x) \\\\  & =-{x}^{3}+2x \\\\  & =-({x}^{3}-2x) \\\\  & =-h(x)\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, by symmetry, we can conclude that\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{-\\pi }^{\\pi }({x}^{3}-2x)\\,\\text{d}x=0.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and III only.\""} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186354654 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186157397 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "3a247336-9bc8-5799-9956-e3de188ce2d2" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Integrals That Evaluate to Zero" :topic/_knowledge-points [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048495 :topic/id #uuid "a4841cae-d95f-5e07-960c-2030a42a2568" :topic/math-academy-id 2975 :topic/title "Evaluating Definite Integrals Using Symmetry"}]}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "44cdd7ee-61ad-5938-93f0-340f091e13f5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186469482} {:answer/id #uuid "2bdca250-cb7c-59e2-8ea4-8682367b7687" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186469483}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "44cdd7ee-61ad-5938-93f0-340f091e13f5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186469482} :answer-field/id #uuid "56a40e4d-8106-5c75-b603-169710891875" :answer-field/key "I" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186470353} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "53d81538-ca99-5b08-9638-204451c7b66d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186469484} {:answer/id #uuid "90b10bca-840e-519e-8eb8-431a9966008b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186469485}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "53d81538-ca99-5b08-9638-204451c7b66d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186469484} :answer-field/id #uuid "a165ae9a-dab8-5535-8ea8-37b5c150931d" :answer-field/key "II" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186470354} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "65aee6b8-9170-5390-9af5-9e119052d466" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186469486} {:answer/id #uuid "db143423-4313-5997-a91e-0fe80488518d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186469487}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "db143423-4313-5997-a91e-0fe80488518d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186469487} :answer-field/id #uuid "0810f300-441d-560e-bef7-e416184b61d6" :answer-field/key "III" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186470355}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "ebd12802-ac6e-5b21-a42e-2abc0f87cbb7" :question/math-academy-id "q-88689" :question/problem "Which of the following integrals evaluates to zero due to symmetry?\n- **I.** ${\\int }_{-4}^{4}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3x)\\,\\text{d}x$\n- **II.** ${\\int }_{-2}^{4}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x)\\,\\text{d}x$\n- **III.** ${\\int }_{-1}^{1}2\\operatorname{tan}⁡x\\,\\text{d}x$\n\nSelect all that apply." :question/worked-solution "Recall the following:\n\n- a function $f(x)$ is *even* if $f(-x)=f(x)$\n\n- a function $f(x)$ is *odd* if $f(-x)=-f(x)$\n\nMoreover, for any odd function $f(x),$ the graph of $y=f(x)$ has rotational symmetry of order $2$ about the origin, so we have\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{-a}^{a}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x=0.\n$$\n\nSo, to determine which integrals evaluate to $0$ due to symmetry, we have to determine which integrands are odd functions.\n\n- The function $f(x)=\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3x)$ is *not* an odd function:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(-x) & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3(-x)) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡(-3x) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3x) \\\\  & \\ne -f(x)\\,\\times \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- The function $g(x)=\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x)$ is an odd function, but integral II is not of the form ${\\int }_{-a}^{a}g(x)\\,\\text{d}x$ since the upper and lower limits are not opposites. Therefore, we cannot use symmetry to simplify the integral.\n\n- The function $h(x)=2\\operatorname{tan}⁡x$ is an odd function:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}h(-x) & =2\\operatorname{tan}⁡(-x) \\\\  & =-2\\operatorname{tan}⁡x \\\\  & =-h(x)\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, by symmetry, we can conclude that\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{-1}^{1}2\\operatorname{tan}⁡x\\,\\text{d}x=0.\n$$\n\nHence, the correct answer is \"III only.\""} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186354655 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186157397 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "3a247336-9bc8-5799-9956-e3de188ce2d2" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Integrals That Evaluate to Zero" :topic/_knowledge-points [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048495 :topic/id #uuid "a4841cae-d95f-5e07-960c-2030a42a2568" :topic/math-academy-id 2975 :topic/title "Evaluating Definite Integrals Using Symmetry"}]}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "528a789d-e9c7-5c1e-bede-584c406a35b3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 1759